Certainly! And yes, they would, and they do. Please bear in mind this isn't compilable code as I haven't actually written this yet, I am just telling you how I would do this, given the previous times I have needed to write such a beast. First of all though, the reason why.

The naive approach is to simply have a slice of slices. If the process writes 20 characters, you add another "chunk" that is 20 bytes long to the slice. The reason I didn't do this is, frankly, past experience. Lets say your process was going to produce 1MB of image data. It did it incrementally as it computed each bit. For the sake of argument lets say that the smallest chunk that your algorithm can produce the image at is 128 bytes. Again for the sake of simplicity lets assume the writer always got full chunks. That means it would take 8192 chunks to save the image. Each slice in Go takes 24 bytes of memory just for housekeeping, not counting any storage space. That means the overhead of using a slice per chunk is 20%!!! Thats huge, even on systems with lots of memory that's 192KB of memory just on slice overhead. PER READER! By using 4K chunks that reduces just the slice overhead down from 192K to 6K, and that's not even counting the pressure relief on the GC. So the principle of chunking is a sound one.

As for what the reader needs to do, please realize I am typing this off the top of my head as a response this is not from running code so please dont expect this to compile. Its just the concepts here.

There is a reader struct that looks something like this:
```
type logReader struct {
  l  *MemoryLogger
  chunkIdx int // Which chunk are we dealing with
  chunkOffs int // How far into the chunk are we?
}
```

We give this a Read() method so that this can satisfy io.Reader. Please note that the whole reader logic is more than this (bounds checking etc):
```
func (rdr *logReader) Read(buf []byte) (int, error) // int may not be the correct type, I will check
{
    // NOT DONE: Checking rdr for bounds. Can use l == nil as a signal for EOF.
    for {
        rdr.l.mu.Lock()
        // NOT DONE: If we've reached the end of the current chunk, increase the index.
        if rdr.chunkIdx < len(rdr.l.chunks) {
            // Save the current chunk
            saved := rdr.l.chunks[rdr.chunkIdx]
            start := rdr.chunkOffs
             end := start + len(buf)
            // Make sure we dont step over the end of the chunk
            if end > len(saved) {
                end = len(saved)
            }
            rdr.l.mu.Unlock()

            // At this point we have a private copy of the chunk we're currently reading from
            // so nothing can touch it and it will be discarded by the GC soon anyway. But the
            // but the lock is released so this reader cant stall any other readers, or the writer.
            // The sum total of work done with the lock held was saving 1 chunk and 1 index.

            copied := copy(buf, saved[start:end])
            rdr.chunkOffs += copied
            return copied, nil
        }

        rdr.l.cond.Wait()
        rdr..l.mu.Unlock()
    }
}
```
Please forgive the sloppy formatting, the GH web textarea input is no Goland, I assure you :)

So yes, you save a chunk and a pointer, per reader. That is CONSIDERABLY less memory than simply doing byte slice appends. You can get better or worse performance depending on the actual pattern of data, but 4K (which I selected out of habit back when memory pages were 4K) is reasonable. Too big, and you pay a memory cost if there are lots of readers. Too small and you pay the cost of excessive GC churn. Mind you the modern go GC is fairly good so perhaps I am worrying too much, but I cant unlearn things that have bitten me in the past, and this exact pattern has certainly bit me before! so I chose the safer (and more performant) approach over the simple 1 or 2 liner it would be if you just kept on appending to a slice of byte slices.

